# What a WPT number here counts, declared rather than described.
#
# ROADMAP §B19.2. §B13.3 records the caveat this file exists to replace: 70% of
# every passing subtest comes from twenty files, all of them one behaviour
# (encode a character into a URL) repeated once per codepoint. That is true, it
# was correctly stated, and it lived only in a paragraph of prose — so it
# travelled exactly as far as somebody reading that paragraph. A headline of
# "333,690 subtests passing" carried none of it.
#
# A tiers file makes the caveat structural. The encoding directory becomes one
# tier among several instead of a footnote, and a reader checks the claim by
# reading a table rather than by trusting us.
#
# ## The rule this file is built on
#
# **Exclusion is by capability, never by outcome.** A directory is out because
# this engine does not implement the thing it tests, and the reason is on the
# line. A directory is never out because it scores badly. Anyone can check that
# by moving a line and re-running: if a tier's number jumps, the line was doing
# the wrong job.
#
# The exclusions are not invented here. §B6 decided what this engine is not,
# years before there was an instrument that would use the decision — no
# iframes, no popups, no workers, no media pipeline, no hardware — and this file
# is that decision written in the form a report can read.
#
# ## Format
#
#   <tier> <prefix>            one rule per line, first match wins
#
# A test matches when its path equals <prefix> or starts with "<prefix>/".
# Rules are read top to bottom, so specific exclusions come before the broad
# tier globs. A test matching nothing falls into the **unscoped remainder**,
# which is counted and printed and never folded into a percentage.
#
# ## Tiers
#
#   core       what an agent reading a page depends on: parse the document,
#              run its script, resolve URLs, fetch subresources, read the DOM
#              back. The headline. A regression here is a product regression.
#   relevant   JS-observable correctness that needs no layout, no media and no
#              second browsing context. Reported as core + relevant.
#   encoding   **its own tier, and that is the whole point.** It is real
#              engineering (§B13.1: a legacy Japanese page now reads correctly)
#              and it is 220,000 subtests of one behaviour. Folded into `core`
#              it would swamp every other capability; dropped it would hide
#              work that was done. So it is named, and the report prints core
#              with and without it.
#   exclude    out of scope by capability, with the reason on the line.
#
# There is deliberately **no "full" tier**. Obscura publishes one and it invites
# the reading their own header warns against: a "60% of everything" that mixes
# what an engine cannot do with what it does badly. The unscoped remainder is
# printed as a count instead, so the denominator is always visible and never
# rounded into a score.

# ---- Excluded: no second browsing context (§B6) -----------------------------
# No iframes and no popups, so anything whose subject is a second context is
# testing a thing this engine refuses to have.
exclude html/browsers/windows
exclude html/browsers/the-window-object
exclude html/browsers/browsing-the-web
exclude html/browsers/history
exclude html/browsers/sandboxing
# Decided rather than discovered, 2026-08-28 (§B20.15). Three subtrees kept
# falling out of every work queue for the same two reasons, so the reasons are
# now on the line here instead of in a caveat nobody reads:
#
#   * origin isolation tests need several live browsing contexts talking to
#     each other, which is the capability §B6 refuses — two origins in one
#     realm is the hazard the cookie jar's retain-on-navigation rule exists to
#     bound, and h5i's answer to "a second page" is a second session.
exclude html/browsers/origin
#   * fetch/metadata observes Sec-Fetch-* headers through window.open plus
#     wptserve .py handlers. **The feature is still wanted** — this engine IS
#     the HTTP client and should send these headers for real sites — it is the
#     tests that need a capability the engine refuses. Excluding the tests does
#     not excuse the feature.
exclude fetch/metadata
#   * gethtml.html is 6,528 subtests of serialising a shadow tree as
#     `<template shadowrootmode>` markup. A shadow root here is a view of its
#     host by design — the flattening the accessibility tree performs, and the
#     shape the snapshot is built on — so the string a browser would produce
#     describes a tree this engine deliberately does not have. One file,
#     excluded by exact path so the rest of shadow-dom stays measured.
exclude shadow-dom/declarative/gethtml.html
#
# Two things that look like candidates and are NOT excluded, deliberately:
# the form-submission enctype files (their *subject* is entry-list
# serialisation, which this engine claims — only the harness observes it
# through an iframe), and the fetch abort timeouts (an implementation ceiling
# §B20.15 reclassifies as a performance item, not a declared boundary).
# Excluding either would be exclusion by outcome wearing a capability's name.
exclude html/semantics/embedded-content/the-iframe-element
exclude html/semantics/embedded-content/the-object-element
exclude html/semantics/embedded-content/the-embed-element

# ---- Excluded: no workers (§B6) ---------------------------------------------
exclude workers
exclude service-workers
exclude shared-workers
exclude worklets
exclude background-fetch
exclude background-sync
exclude push-api
exclude notifications

# ---- CSS: the parts with a scriptable surface, before the broad exclusion ---
# Read first, because first match wins and the catch-all below would otherwise
# swallow them. These three are what an agent actually touches: the object model
# over stylesheets, the geometry queries, and selector matching.
core css/cssom
core css/cssom-view
core css/selectors
core css/css-conditional
core css/css-syntax
core css/css-variables

# ---- Excluded: no layout, paint or animation surface ------------------------
# Blitz lays the page out and this engine reads the result, but nothing here
# exposes a layout API to script or drives an animation timeline.
exclude css/css-animations
exclude css/css-transitions
exclude css/motion
exclude web-animations
exclude scroll-animations
exclude css-animations
exclude css-transitions
exclude intersection-observer
exclude resize-observer
exclude layout-instability
exclude visual-viewport
exclude scroll-to-text-fragment
exclude html/rendering
exclude html/canvas
exclude mathml
exclude svg/painting
exclude svg/animations
# Everything else under `css/` is layout, paint, fonts and typography — Blitz
# and Stylo do that work and this engine reads the result rather than exposing
# it to script. 75,000 subtests, and leaving them unscoped made the remainder
# the second-largest row in the table.
exclude css

# ---- Excluded: no input devices ---------------------------------------------
# The engine synthesises the events its own verbs need and nothing else. A
# pointer-event suite is testing hardware this process does not have.
exclude pointerevents
exclude pointerlock
exclude touch-events
exclude uievents/mouse
exclude input-events
exclude editing
exclude html/editing
exclude html/interaction
exclude fullscreen
exclude picture-in-picture

# ---- Excluded: no media pipeline --------------------------------------------
exclude media-source
exclude mediacapture-streams
exclude mediacapture-record
exclude mediacapture-image
exclude mediacapture-fromelement
exclude mediasession
exclude media-capabilities
exclude media-playback-quality
exclude encrypted-media
exclude webaudio
exclude webcodecs
exclude webvtt
exclude remote-playback
exclude presentation-api

# ---- Excluded: no GPU, no hardware, no device APIs --------------------------
exclude webgl
exclude webgpu
exclude webrtc
exclude webrtc-encoded-transform
exclude webrtc-extensions
exclude webrtc-stats
exclude webrtc-svc
exclude webxr
exclude webusb
exclude web-bluetooth
exclude bluetooth
exclude web-nfc
exclude webauthn
exclude gamepad
exclude vibration
exclude accelerometer
exclude ambient-light
exclude gyroscope
exclude magnetometer
exclude orientation-event
exclude compute-pressure
exclude screen-orientation
exclude screen-wake-lock
exclude geolocation
exclude netinfo
exclude battery-status
exclude idle-detection

# ---- Excluded: no chrome around the page ------------------------------------
# There is no UI, so there is nothing for these to act on.
exclude payment-request
exclude payment-handler
exclude credential-management
exclude digital-credentials
exclude clipboard-apis
exclude speech-api
exclude web-share
exclude badging
exclude window-management

# ---- Excluded: timing surfaces this engine reports differently ---------------
# The settle runs on a *virtual* clock (§B15.10), which is the property that
# makes a replay deterministic and is deliberately not what these measure.
exclude paint-timing
exclude largest-contentful-paint
exclude element-timing
exclude long-animation-frame
exclude longtask-timing
exclude navigation-timing
exclude event-timing

# ---- Excluded: storage and platform surfaces this engine does not have ------
# §B6 commits web storage to memory, so there is no storage engine for an
# indexed database to sit on, and no origin-scoped filesystem.
exclude IndexedDB
exclude fs
exclude file-system-access
exclude web-locks
exclude cookiestore
exclude storage-access-api
# No second browsing context, so nothing to message between, and no navigation
# API over a history this engine does not keep.
exclude webmessaging
exclude navigation-api
exclude fenced-frame
exclude close-watcher
exclude soft-navigation-heuristics
# Transports and platform APIs with no implementation here.
exclude webtransport
exclude webnn
exclude ai
exclude fedcm
exclude beacon
exclude selection
exclude resource-timing
exclude connection-allowlist
exclude webmcp

# ---- The encoding tier: named, so a headline cannot hide inside it ----------
encoding encoding
encoding encoding-detection

# ---- Core: what an agent reading a page depends on --------------------------
core dom
core domparsing
core domxpath
core html
core url
core urlpattern
core fetch
core xhr
core cors
core webstorage
core custom-elements
core shadow-dom
core mimesniff
core css/cssom
core css/cssom-view
core css/selectors

# ---- Relevant: JS-observable correctness needing none of the above ----------
relevant webidl
relevant streams
relevant compression
relevant FileAPI
relevant uievents
relevant trusted-types
relevant sanitizer-api
relevant WebCryptoAPI
relevant hr-time
relevant user-timing
relevant performance-timeline
relevant console
relevant referrer-policy
relevant content-security-policy
relevant subresource-integrity
relevant secure-contexts
relevant cookies
relevant storage
relevant websockets
relevant eventsource
relevant import-maps
relevant js
relevant ecmascript
relevant wai-aria
relevant accname
relevant html-aam
relevant page-visibility
relevant requestidlecallback
relevant permissions-policy
relevant svg
relevant wasm
